Context menus: do not select the item the button is released over

GTK's menus follow the X11 press-drag-release model: pressing opens the
menu and releasing over an item chooses it, so one press completes the
selection. Windows uses two steps — releasing opens the menu and leaves
it there, and a second, separate click chooses.

Someone arriving from Windows lets go of the right button as soon as the
menu appears and lands on whatever item is under the pointer. GTK3
already guards the first 500ms against exactly this, which is an
admission that the model surprises people; this raises the guard past
any plausible press so that release never selects. Dragging inside an
already-open menu is unaffected.

Applies to: gtk+3.0 3.24.52

--- a/gtk/gtkmenushell.c
+++ b/gtk/gtkmenushell.c
@@ -84,7 +84,13 @@
 #include "ubuntu-private.h"
 
 
-#define MENU_SHELL_TIMEOUT   500
+/* Windows opens a context menu on release and leaves it there; the
+ * release that opened it never selects anything. GTK's own guard for
+ * the first MENU_SHELL_TIMEOUT milliseconds does the same thing for a
+ * quick press-and-release, so raising it past any plausible press gives
+ * the Windows model without touching the surrounding logic.
+ */
+#define MENU_SHELL_TIMEOUT   2147483
 #define MENU_POPUP_DELAY     225
 #define MENU_POPDOWN_DELAY   1000
 
